 years  left on our Sun so we don't have to  worry about that event happening  imminently and the question of how much  longer this universe goes on is one that  the theologia theologists can argue  about with the cosmologists but that is  the interesting intersection that we  reach in the end of don't know much  about the universe because now we have  the scientists talking about what the  theologist the theologians only used to  talk about  it's that intersection between faith and  reason science and religion that I find  so fascinating thank you please your  subjects are so broad how do you begin  to structure your research when you're  getting started how do you how do you  tackle it my subjects are so broad how  do I attack them well as Michael said  when he introduced me I consider myself  a very average person and I think that  if I'm interested in something a lot of  other people will be interested in it  but it basically comes down to two  things curiosity and sort of what I call  household word approach we all have  these words whether it's in the field of  astronomy or American history or  geography or the Bible we have these  words and phrases and people and events  that we all recall as being important  but we can't always put our finger on  exactly why so the first thing I do is  really look for those household words  whether it's black hole in don't know  much about the universe or manifest  destiny in talking about American  history and if you can find a way to  talk about those those terms those  phrases in a way that's accessible means  something to people today and not just a  phrase that relates back to what  happened 200 years ago or is only of  interest to a few scientists I think  that's when you connect with the average  person's curiosity I've found in the  past 10 years and 11 years that I've  been doing these books and traveling  around the country and speaking to  people in sessions like this or on the  raid  that there is enormous appetite for  learning people want to know they want  to learn they want to understand but  they certainly wanted in a style of  format that's a lot more appealing than  the way most of us got it back in high  school I can't tell you how many  teachers people have said to me they  learned history from the football coach  I hope there are no football coaches who  teach American history here today but  I've heard that story so many times it  was not the case in in my education but  I've heard it often enough to think well  gee there might be a pattern here there  might be a problem that a guy who you  know needed something teaches football  but needs something else to kind of  occupy his day well they put them in the  history class maybe that's the way it  works in some parts of the country I  don't know but but we need to keep  learning and I think that just answering  these questions in a way that is a  little offbeat and fun and entertaining  it's not for everyone my books are not  considered you know Pulitzer Prize  material add that's ok as long as I keep  getting letters from kids who say I  hated history until I read your book and  I don't now a real or a parent who says  we took a trip and we listened to your  book about American history on our honor  on the road and really allowed us to  talk about the places we've been this is  partly a book these are books about  starting conversations they are not the  last word on these subjects sometimes  they're the first word and then I want  you to go out and keep finding out more  I'll try and give you the basic tools  and the basic information so you can  have an intelligent conversation about  these things but there's a big  difference between the subtitle says  everything you need to know and  everything there is to know it's a  somewhat subjective idea and I'm sure  there are people who think I've left  very important things out of some of my  books but for the main I just try and  cover the basic basis and I'll tell y