 aha Eureka  moments that I so often come across in  my research and that is one of the  reasons I do my own research I there is  not you know a stable of 100 little  assistants scurrying around I have the  luxury of working on these books and  these books alone  I don't have to teach or wait tables or  drive a cab as so many other writers  have to do to earn a living so I have  the luxury of working on each of these  books for about three years and fairly  intensively cramming and I guess what  would be you know a couple of years of  graduate school worth of research and  study in a very short space of time so  that's my basic approach and then that  the trick is always to find a way to  make it a little bit more fun the  information is out there this stuff is  in all these books people have spent  their lives finding the stuff and trying  to tell us about it some of them are not  so good at telling us about so I try and  be you know the guy who finds all the  interesting stuff and then pass it along  to you another question sir yes sir you  talked about writing and perhaps  popularizing science in some way and we  we all know that in this country science  and schools is being under taught Carl  Sagan was one of those people who also  popularize science do you see another  Carl Sagan out there somewhere someone  who can make science entertaining gee  let me think about that is there  somebody out there who can make  astronomy entertaining I'll have to  think about that awhile I think there  are a lot of really good writers in the  science field Carl Sagan was a singular  person and he had this extraordinary  showman's ability and being a good  teacher is sometimes being a bit of a  showman I've always thought that  teaching was you know partly theatre  partly stand-up comedy and partly really  know what knowing what you're talking  about and the best teachers and I always  believed that the reason you really  learn something is school in school is  because you have